The next thing to verify

We need to determine who is keeping the node cordoned. Please run:

oc describe node sno1.lab1.ocp.lan | tail -50

and

oc get events -A --sort-by=.lastTimestamp | grep -Ei "cordon|drain|unsched|sriov|machineconfig"

Also, inspect the detailed SR-IOV node state:

oc describe sriovnetworknodestate sno1.lab1.ocp.lan \
-n openshift-sriov-network-operator

Pay particular attention to the Status, Conditions, and Events sections.

If nothing is actively draining the node

If the outputs show there is no active drain in progress, you can try:

oc adm uncordon sno1.lab1.ocp.lan

Then immediately watch:

watch oc get nodes

or

oc get nodes -w

If the node becomes SchedulingDisabled again within a few seconds, then some controller (most likely the SR-IOV config daemon) is re-cordoning it, and the describe sriovnetworknodestate output should reveal why.

One more thing I noticed

Your operator pod is Pending because the node is unschedulable:

0/1 nodes are available: 1 node(s) were unschedulable

Until the node is successfully uncordoned, the SR-IOV operator Deployment cannot run, which also prevents the CSV from progressing from Installing to Succeeded.

The outputs of the three commands above should identify the controller or condition that is keeping the node in the Draining state.
